{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792269256,"nonce":"FXR6jVVwjinhgslhocBpZKeszus=","title":"Layout widgets cannot ship their own default CSS","message":"cn ships its design as `CN_STYLES`, delivered through the theme bundle. A widget in blinc_layout has no equivalent: the code editor's find bar is designed by `CODE_EDITOR_STYLES`, but an app only gets it by adding it by hand (`ctx.add_css`) or by using `cn_bundle()`; without it the bar falls back to a plain look. There is no per-element or per-tree stylesheet to carry it, and no registry a widget can add a default sheet to.\n\nWanted: layout widgets register a default stylesheet (lowest precedence, parsed with the theme's variables, re-parsed on a theme change) that the runner layers under the app's own CSS, so classed layout widgets are designed out of the box and apps override after. Touches each runner's stylesheet setup (windowed, web, iOS, Android) and the theme-change reparse, so it needs a pass over all four.\n\nUntil then `CODE_EDITOR_STYLES` is exported from blinc_layout::widgets, included by blinc_cn::cn_bundle, and documented in the book's code editor page.","files":null}]}